notes.body:
> In this case it means, that the default themes that Qt uses on Linux are mostly only light-mode based and not capable of switching between modes. There are major improvements lately but in general switching through Qt is not possible. I assume the same goes for any other non-Windows and macOS system (just guessing, have not tried).

According to [the documentation for the `platformName` method for the `QGuiApplication` class](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qguiapplication.html#platformName-prop), the GUI platforms they have are:

- Android (which I sometimes call "Bionic/Linux" :-));
- Cocoa (which means "the macOS GUI");
- DirectFB (which means "directly drawing on the frame buffer; I don't know if DirectFB is only a Linux interface or if other OSes have adopted it);
- EGLFS (apparently "run atop [EGL](https://www.khronos.org/egl) and OpenGL");
- iOS ("also used for tvOS", and maybe also used on iPadOS, although treating iOS and iPadOS as equivalent isn't always the right thing to do)
- Linux frame buffer via fbdev;
- "minimal" (to be used as a template for new platform implementations, and also supports "[running] GUI applications in environments without a GUI, such as servers";
- "minimal EGL" (another sample implementation, presumably involving the aforementioned EGL);
- "offscreen", whatever *that* is;
- QNX;
- Windows;
- Wayland;
- XGL, meaning "X11 using the xgl X11 client library".

So, on desktop Linux, the backend is either XGL or Wayland; the same applies to most other UN\*Xes on desktops.

The problem for those is that the low-level window system is just the beginning of the story; there are multiple desktop environments. The main desktop environments run on OSes other than Linux - my FreeBSD and OpenBSD VMs use GNOME, and the first PC I did development on mostly ran KDE on FreeBSD.

So:

- "Linux", in this context, mainly means "some X11 or Wayland-based desktop environment;
- "light" and "dark" modes may be interpreted differently on different desktop environments;

and there may be more commonality between, say, GNOME on Linux and GNOME on FreeBSD than between GNOME on Linux and KDE Plasma on Linux and Xfce on Linux and "I just run xterm and [ratpoison](https://www.nongnu.org/ratpoison/)" on Linux.

Maybe someday freedesktop.org will come up with a standard for choosing dark vs. light mode and all the major desktop environments will implement it. (And maybe someday monkeys will fly....)

If we cannot make the preview closer to actual look I think we should be smarter and revert to something closer to the old look. Having one "packet" for each of the different examples with a clear text what it is may be easier to understand. I cannot figure how to configure the colors like I used to do, maybe this also should be changed back to the old behavior?

Here the colors are wrong, fonts are wrong, packet details wrong, packet bytes wrong, status bar wrong order. It's also hard to understand what is marked and ignored examples.
![Screenshot_2026-05-30_at_21.44.40](/uploads/bd91223d6f9061a272ec38b3eb647e92/Screenshot_2026-05-30_at_21.44.40.png){width=596 height=358}

